# References

A reference is a link to something named in the workspace: a heading, a figure, a table, a listing, an anchor, or a page. It shows what it points to — a number, a title — and follows it: when the target is renumbered, renamed or moved, the reference changes with it.

:example
```
## Setup {#setup}

:figure #plan The plan
![](/media/diagram.svg)

[[setup]] and [[plan]].
```

## Forms

| Reference | Points to |
|:----------|:----------|
| `[[name]]` | `name` in this page, or else the only one of the workspace with that name. |
| `[[page#name]]` | `name` in the page `page`. |
| `[[page#]]` | The page `page` itself. |
| `[[#name]]` | `name` anywhere in the workspace: it must be unique. |

A page is named by its file's name without `.nml`: `writing/references.nml` is `references`. A page can choose another name with the variable `nml.reference_key` (see [[variables#]]).

A reference to nothing, or to a name several pages use, is an error that says which.

## Captions

Without a caption, a reference shows its target's label: *Figure 2* for a figure, the title for a heading or a page. A caption, after `|`, replaces it, and can show the target's `{title}`, `{number}` and `{label}`:

:example
```
:figure #cell A cell
![](/media/diagram.svg)

See [[cell|figure {number}]], or
[[cell|the one titled “{title}”]].
```

## Anchors {#anchor}

`:anchor` marks a place to refer to, with an optional title, where there is nothing else to name:

:example
```
:anchor defaults The default settings

The defaults are below.

[[defaults]] links here.
```
